    level: "B1",
    text: "Humans experience the world through five senses: sight, hearing, smell, taste, and touch. Sight, or vision, is often considered the most dominant sense. The eyes detect light, color, and movement, allowing us to see the world around us. Hearing, also called audition, lets us perceive sound waves through the ears. We can distinguish between loud and quiet sounds, high-pitched and low-pitched tones, and the direction from which sound comes. Smell, known scientifically as olfaction, is processed through the nose. The sense of smell is closely linked to memory and emotion — a familiar scent can instantly transport you to a childhood moment. Taste, or gustation, works through taste buds on the tongue. We can detect five basic tastes: sweet, sour, salty, bitter, and umami (savory). Taste and smell work closely together, which is why food seems bland when you have a cold. Touch, the sense of tactile perception, covers the entire body through the skin. Receptors in the skin detect pressure, temperature, pain, and texture. Some areas, like the fingertips, are extremely sensitive, while others, like the back, are less so. Together, these senses help us navigate, communicate, and stay safe in our environment.",
